# Build output
build/
build-*/

# PER-LANE BUILD DIRECTORIES. Sixteen of these were committed once -- 135 MB of
# compiled test binaries in a repository whose entire history otherwise packs to
# 784 KB -- and they got in because they are named after the lane that produced
# them and so matched neither `build/` nor `build-*/`.
#
# `bl-` IS A PATTERN AND `v-` IS A LIST, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS DELIBERATE. An
# ignore rule that is too broad fails silently and in the expensive direction:
# it does not add a file, it SUBTRACTS one, and the day someone adds a source
# directory the rule happens to match, the file is simply never staged and
# nothing says so. `bl-` is a prefix nothing else here would want. `v-` is not
# -- `v2/`, `vendor/`, `version/` all start that way -- so the eight that exist
# are named and a ninth will have to be named too. Being told to add a line is a
# better failure than losing a file quietly.
bl-*/
/v-clang-address/
/v-clang-none/
/v-clang-thread/
/v-clang-undefined/
/v-gcc-16-address/
/v-gcc-16-none/
/v-gcc-16-thread/
/v-gcc-16-undefined/

CMakeCache.txt
CMakeFiles/
cmake_install.cmake
CTestTestfile.cmake
Testing/
compile_commands.json
*.a
*.o
*.so
*.dylib

# Engine roots written at a RELATIVE path by a test, so they land wherever the
# lane ran. test/tsan_harness_test.cc hardcodes this one and leaves the
# directory behind.
/tsan-flush-db/

# Editors / OS
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
